\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces System block diagram. An input image from CIFAR-10 or MNIST passes through the THz-like degradation pipeline, is normalized to the ImageNet distribution, is fed to one of the three backbones, and is classified by a global-average-pooling head.}}{7}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces Pipeline version 2. The saturation lerp runs first so the grayscale luminance is computed from the un-degraded source. Additive noise and salt-and-pepper operate at the pre-upsample resolution; bicubic upsample spreads each stochastic sample over the kernel footprint, producing sensor-grain noise structure rather than fine pixel-level grain.}}{8}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces CIFAR-10: six fixed validation examples (deterministic seed) at clean, L1 Mild, L3 Moderate, and L5 Extreme combined degradation. Class labels above each column. Rows from top to bottom: Clean, L1, L3, L5.}}{12}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces CIFAR-10: per-axis isolation at L3 (Phase C). Each row shows the same six images with only the named axis active.}}{14}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces MNIST: combined degradation at L1, L3, L5 on six fixed validation examples.}}{15}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces MNIST: per-axis isolation at L3 (Phase C).}}{16}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces Validation accuracy vs combined degradation level. Solid curves: Phase B (no extra regularization). Dashed curves: Phase D-T3 (T3 regularization on the same pipeline). Dotted horizontal lines: Phase A clean baseline per model.}}{16}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces Validation accuracy vs mean PSNR per pipeline. Each marker is one trained cell; color indicates the backbone. The strong negative monotone relationship justifies treating PSNR as a low-cost proxy for downstream accuracy at design time.}}{17}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces Validation accuracy vs mean SSIM per pipeline.}}{17}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces Per-axis attribution at L3: accuracy delta vs the Phase A clean baseline. Resolution dominates on CIFAR-10; on MNIST the spread between axes is smaller because the digit shapes are tolerant of color loss and moderate blur.}}{18}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces Phase C per-axis accuracy versus image-quality on CIFAR-10. Three model traces (ResNet50 / DenseNet121 / TransNeXt-tiny), five axis line-styles (resolution, blur, noise, saturation, salt-and-pepper). Resolution carries the strongest accuracy-PSNR association (Spearman $\rho = +0.93$); saturation and salt-and-pepper are largely flat.}}{19}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces Phase C per-axis accuracy versus image-quality on MNIST. The resolution axis dominates (Spearman $\rho = +0.98$ vs PSNR); MNIST's near-saturation accuracy at moderate PSNR explains the smaller dynamic range visible here.}}{19}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces Phase D recovery summary. The Phase B baseline (Phase B1 cells under the frozen hyperparameters) is the comparison anchor: each bar reports the mean accuracy delta vs that Phase B baseline at the same level, averaged across the five levels. T3 is the best treatment but the absolute recovery remains modest (mean $\sim 0.77$~percentage points{}) compared with the $-12.49$~percentage points{} Phase B collapse against the earlier pipeline.}}{21}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces Phase B2 protocol-simplification decomposition summary. The B1 to B2 accuracy gain ($\sim +4.01$ percentage points{} on average) decomposes as $\sim +3.24$ percentage points{} pure-protocol effect (saturation and noise off) and $\sim +0.77$ percentage points{} regularization effect.}}{23}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces Phase C2 per-axis THz attribution summary. Mean accuracy delta from L1 to L5: resolution $-27.45$~percentage points{}, blur $-6.58$~percentage points{}, salt and pepper $-5.13$~percentage points{}. Resolution is the dominant axis by a factor of approximately four.}}{24}{figure.caption.32}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces Phase C2 per-axis accuracy versus image-quality on CIFAR-10. Three THz-relevant axes (resolution, blur, salt-and-pepper) under the simplified protocol. Resolution dominates (Spearman $\rho = +0.98$ vs PSNR).}}{24}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces Phase C2 per-axis accuracy versus image-quality on MNIST. Same THz subset; resolution Spearman $\rho = +0.98$ versus PSNR.}}{24}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces Multi-seed variance at L3: each bar reports the mean validation accuracy across seeds $\{42, 43, 44\}$ with the standard-deviation error bar. Color encodes the phase. The $\sigma $ bars are small relative to the cross-phase deltas, which validates the cross-phase comparisons.}}{25}{figure.caption.36}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces Reliability diagrams at Phase B L3. Top row: CIFAR-10. Bottom row: MNIST. Columns: ResNet50, DenseNet121, TransNeXt-tiny.}}{26}{figure.caption.37}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {20}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces Reliability diagrams at Phase B L5. Top row: CIFAR-10. Bottom row: MNIST. Columns: ResNet50, DenseNet121, TransNeXt-tiny.}}{27}{figure.caption.38}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {21}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces Confusion matrices at Phase B L5. Top row: CIFAR-10. Bottom row: MNIST. Columns: ResNet50, DenseNet121, TransNeXt-tiny.}}{28}{figure.caption.39}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces Inference latency per image at $224 \times 224$, batch size 1, \texttt {bf16-mixed}, RTX 5070.}}{29}{figure.caption.40}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {23}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{english}\ignorespaces Planned (top) versus actual (bottom) project schedule. The actual schedule added three late-stage expansion phases (Phase D regularization sweep, Phase B2 / B2-nr / C2 THz-protocol decomposition, and the multi-seed audit) on top of the original plan.}}{34}{figure.caption.42}%
